# CSS Standards

If the project has a styleguide (see theming rule), its tokens and patterns override anything here.

## Selectors and naming
- Semantic class names describing purpose, not appearance: `.btn-primary`, `.nav-item` — never `.big-red-text`.
- Lowercase with hyphens; no camelCase, no underscores, no over-qualified selectors (`.container`, not `div.container`).
- Keep specificity low and flat: no ID selectors for styling, nesting max 3 levels, no `!important` except to override third-party inline styles (comment why).

## Values and properties
- Design tokens / custom properties over hardcoded values; a repeated hex color or magic pixel value is a missing token.
- Shorthand where it doesn't accidentally reset other properties (`margin: 0 auto`, but `margin-left` when overriding one side).
- Unitless `line-height` and zero values (`margin: 0`, not `0px`); numeric font weights (`700`, not `bold`); leading zero for decimals (`0.5`).
- Lowercase hex, shortened where possible (`#fff`); `rgba()`/`color-mix()` when opacity is needed.

## Structure
- Group styles by component/section, consistently ordered properties within a block (display → position → box model → typography → color → other).
- Media queries live next to the component they modify, mobile-first (`min-width` scaling up).
- One selector per line; comments explain *why* (workarounds, stacking contexts, browser quirks), with a table of contents only for long framework-free stylesheets.

## Maintainability
- Don't restate defaults (`display: block` on a `div`).
- Target the element you style directly, not through parent chains that break when the DOM moves.
- Before adding an override, try removing the rule that forces it.
- Preprocessor usage (SCSS/PostCSS): variables and mixins for repetition, partials per component, no deep `@import` chains.
